akr 0.1
project templartorrent

record templartorrent.decision.fork-rbit-for-native-gate/1 : decision {
    title "Fork rbit 0.2.2 in-tree to drop reqwest and ring; announce HTTPS with rustls 0.23.46"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "Cargo.toml", path "integration/rbit-adapter/**", path "vendor/rbit/**" ]
    topic rbit-fork
    decision """
        Vendor rbit 0.2.2 into the repository as a path dependency applied through [patch.crates-io], make its reqwest dependency optional behind a Cargo feature that TemplarTorrent does not enable, and announce with an application-owned bounded HTTP/1.1 client that connects to the already-admitted tracker address. HTTPS announces use rustls 0.23.46, pinned, with default features off and only std, tls12 and aws_lc_rs enabled. That is the cryptography provider the 2026-10-14 rustls release enables by default. ring is not enabled. Certificate verification uses the webpki public roots and is not disabled. Redirects stay refused and the 64 KiB response cap stays.
        """
    context """
        The strict native-source audit reported ring 0.17.14 through rbit -> reqwest (rustls-tls) -> rustls -> ring. The owner chose the fork on 2026-10-22 over accepting ring. On 2026-10-22 the owner then chose the recently updated rustls repository (stable release 0.23.46) for HTTPS trackers. That release's default provider is aws-lc-rs, which builds aws-lc (C, via cmake) rather than ring. rustls-rustcrypto remains the experimental provider the audit already rejected. Brotli certificate decompression stays off; 0.23.46's advisory GHSA-h2v8-394m-p7vv is about that disabled feature.
        """
    consequences """
        The adapter still depends on the rbit API surface, and rbit upgrades require re-applying the feature gate. HTTP and HTTPS tracker announces are the application's responsibility. aws-lc-sys is now an accepted native dependency for tracker TLS only; cargo tree -i ring and -i reqwest stay empty, and the strict all-Rust claim does not cover aws-lc. WebSeed and UPnP from rbit stay unavailable.
        """
    resolves [ @templartorrent.question.ring-release-gate ]
}

record templartorrent.decision.http-download-manager/1 : decision {
    title "Extend the client into an HTTP(S) file download manager on the existing transport"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "DESIGN.md",
        path "crates/templartorrent-core/src/bandwidth.rs",
        path "integration/**"
    ]
    topic http-downloads
    decision """
        Add URL jobs for ordinary http:// and https:// files to the same application, queue, Add dialog, remove dialog and session store as torrents (DESIGN.md section 11). Downloads run over the application's rustls 0.23.46 transport with certificate name checks, a cap of 5 re-admitted redirects, refusal of non-http schemes and of private, loopback, link-local, carrier-grade NAT, unique-local and metadata addresses unless --allow-local-urls or the matching setting is on. A 206 to a Range probe splits files of at least 1 MiB into 4 parallel parts by default and 8 at most, each charging its 64 KiB read buffer to the global payload budget and its bytes to the shared download token bucket; otherwise one connection. Resume needs a strong validator (quoted non-weak ETag, else Last-Modified at least 60 s older than Date) echoed unchanged by a 206 at the requested offset; a mismatch stops the job with a visible reason and keeps the partial file. A connection is dropped after 30 s without data and retried with bounded backoff; there is no total timeout. Bytes go to a temporary sibling, are synced, optionally checked against a user-supplied SHA-256, then renamed. Unknown sizes render as an em dash. Jobs persist as <key>.url records and are restored on launch.
        """
    context """
        The user asked on 2026-11-03 to extend TemplarTorrent into a download manager for ordinary files, using https://github.com/ekremx25/linux-download-manager (registered as source ldm-readme-v0.3.0) only as a behaviour reference: its transfer rules, not its stack. Before this decision Command::Add accepted a magnet or a .torrent only; the HTTP code in announce.rs and portmap.rs had no Range requests, no file writer, no resume identity and no URL job. rbit's webseed module stays unwired; BEP 19 webseeds are a separate feature.
        """
    consequences """
        DESIGN.md section 1 no longer defines a torrent-only product, but still rules out a webview, a second GUI, a media player, stream capture, content search, a browser extension in this tree and any background service after the window closes. Tauri, WebKit, yt-dlp, FFmpeg, HLS and tray-hide mode from the reference are not adopted. The adapter gains an http module and a sha2 dependency already present in the graph through rbit; the engine's download bucket becomes a shared handle so one limit governs peers and URL connections. URL jobs are admitted as their own pool of the Download jobs size. Proof is localhost fixtures only; Internet transfers stay opt-in. Browser link capture is reviewed in a repository markdown rather than implemented.
        """
    author "Claude"
}

record templartorrent.decision.liminalkit-unpinned/1 : decision {
    title "Take the UI toolkit as liminalkit from its main branch, held by Cargo.lock"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "Cargo.lock",
        path "DESIGN.md",
        path "integration/pixelkit-preview/Cargo.toml",
        path "integration/templartorrent/Cargo.toml"
    ]
    topic ui-toolkit
    decision """
        The client and the preview depend on liminalkit-raster, -shell, -text, -ui and -windowing from https://github.com/liminalism/liminalkit without a rev; Cargo.lock holds the exact commit (19c5cd393ad6ee802170a789fd3fd1e4ff84bcdf at this decision). A toolkit bump is a deliberate lockfile change reviewed for API drift, lockfile diff and a baseline comparison, as DESIGN.md's compatibility gate already required of a rev bump. The pixelkit-preview directory keeps its name.
        """
    context """
        On 2026-11-03 the user said PixelKit no longer needs to be pinned to 614c9c6e84ce3cd0bc6c81988f636b493b30a497: the toolkit has improved since and is now called liminalkit. The API the client uses (Theme, Ui, table, text_field, checkbox, scroll_list, underline_tabs, Panes, PixelApp, Waker, WindowConfig, run_app, Input, Clipboard, ClipboardWorker, FontSet, TextCache) is unchanged at liminalkit 19c5cd3 apart from the crate names, so the move was a rename of the five dependency lines and the crate paths in the client and preview sources.
        """
    consequences """
        Cargo.lock changed by exactly the five toolkit entries; every other dependency is the same version. At 19c5cd3 the client suite passed 38 of 39 (the clipboard test still needs a display), Clippy with warnings denied passed, and the preview's 10 headless tests passed. A baseline comparison of first frame and RSS was not possible in the build container (no display); it is owed on the next measured machine before any release claim. Because main can move, cargo update will take new toolkit commits: that is the reviewed path for bumps, and CI builds with the locked commit.
        """
    author "Claude"
}
